gnome-phone (0.1.10) unstable; urgency=medium

  * neo-dictate: the document is read only, so tapping it no longer brings
    up the on-screen keyboard, and what is said always goes at its end. A
    long press or a double tap selects a word, a drag on from it selects
    word by word, and a bar over the selection copies it or selects
    everything.

 -- pulpoff <pashkovsky@gmail.com>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 09:49:10 +0100

gnome-phone (0.1.9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * neo-dictate: Dictate, a new app. A microphone button and a document:
    what is said appears in it as it is said, recognised on the phone with
    nothing sent anywhere, in English, German, Spanish (Moonshine's tiny
    streaming models, with punctuation and capitals), French, Italian or
    Russian (Vosk's small models, lower case). The app bar's language
    button chooses among the languages installed; Copy and Clear are next
    to it. The recognisers and models are not in these packages:
    tools/neo-dictate-speech/build-debs.sh makes neo-dictate-moonshine,
    neo-dictate-vosk and neo-dictate-en, -de, -es, -fr, -it and -ru from
    their makers' releases.
  * neo-iconpack-round: an icon for Dictate.
  * gnome-shell-extension-gnome-phone: the lock screen's wallpaper is
    decoded off the main loop, at the panel's size instead of three times
    it. At boot its first frame took 2 s on a Galaxy S10e with the splash
    still up; the lock screen now comes about 1.5 s sooner after the
    shell starts.

 -- pulpoff <pashkovsky@gmail.com>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 08:23:16 +0100

gnome-phone (0.1.8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * gnome-shell-extension-gnome-phone: the boot goes from the splash
    straight to the lock screen. The mobile shell used to show its
    overview, top panel and grey background for about 6 s first; with
    patches/gnome-shell/0004 the extension keeps the shell from drawing
    until it has locked, and the display keeps the splash until then. A
    shell without that patch starts as before.
  * gnome-shell-extension-gnome-phone: a swipe unlock shows the launcher
    coming up behind the lock screen instead of going black for 500 ms
    first, and the bouncer's blur blurs that screen instead of black.
  * gnome-shell-extension-gnome-phone: tapping the search field in the app
    drawer no longer moves the whole launcher up behind the keyboard.
  * neo-launcher: the app drawer has no search field by default (Settings >
    Home screen > Layout turns it on). A user who switched it on keeps it.
  * patches/gnome-shell/0004: lets an extension keep the stage hidden while
    GNOME Shell starts (LayoutManager.holdStartup()), for gnome-shell-mobile
    51.1.mobile.22. The shell shows the stage anyway after 15 s.

 -- pulpoff <pashkovsky@gmail.com>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 03:00:00 +0100

gnome-phone (0.1.7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Every ID is neo.* now: the apps (neo.Dialer, neo.Messaging, neo.Clock,
    neo.Calendar, neo.Contacts, neo.Calculator, neo.Camera, neo.Files,
    neo.Compass) with their services and windows, neo.Browser, neo.Weather,
    neo.Settings and neo.Launcher, the shared phone settings (neo.Phone at
    /neo/Phone/), the extension's D-Bus name (neo.GnomePhone) and UUID
    (gnome-phone@neo), neo-wakeup's system bus name (neo.Wakeup), the
    GSettings paths, the GResource prefixes, the desktop files and the
    icons. Device overrides must name [neo.Phone] and the new desktop ids.
  * A separate product's app is no longer part of the default home layout,
    and the Round icon pack no longer has an icon for it.
  * gnome-phone-daemons: gnome-phone-migrate-ids carries each user over
    once, from a systemd user unit before the session starts (the launcher
    runs it too when it finds no stamp): GSettings values are copied to the
    new paths, a device's old-schema overrides that no longer apply are
    kept as the user's values, the apps' own directories (alarms, the
    browser's profile, the calculator's history) move to the new names
    with a symlink left behind, the browser's home screen shortcuts,
    default apps, favourites, notification settings and the enabled
    extension follow. Nothing old is deleted; what was done is listed in
    ~/.local/state/gnome-phone/ids-migrated. Depends on dconf-cli.
  * neo-launcher: reads old desktop ids in a home layout (the user's or a
    ROM's in /etc/xdg) and in hidden-from-home as the new ones once the
    renamed app is installed, and an icon pack's map made for 0.1.6 still
    dresses the renamed apps. A tap on the home screen's clock opens the
    Clock.
  * The home layout survives an upgrade from 0.1.6 made with the phone in
    use: the 0.1.6 launcher still running takes each renamed app for a new
    one and rearranges the icons. The app packages' preinst saves every
    user's layout to /var/lib/neo-launcher/before-0.1.7/ before the first
    app is renamed, and gnome-phone-migrate-ids puts it back once at the
    next login (the rearranged one is kept as layout-upgrade-0.1.7.json).
  * gnome-shell-extension-gnome-phone: the status bar no longer vanishes
    from the home screen after hours of use. Mutter's unredirect count,
    which keeps the launcher's fullscreen window off the display while the
    bar is up, could be left one short by any part of the shell; while
    the extension has anything on screen, the count can no longer reach
    zero.
  * neo-dialer: a key's tone plays when the key is pressed, and only that
    key's tone. The tone stream opens with the keypad and keeps running,
    with a 50 ms buffer, and the dialer makes the two sines itself, 10 ms
    at a time, from the key held at that moment; the stream used to be
    started and stopped on every key behind a 200 ms buffer, so a tone
    came late and began with what was left of the key before.
  * Comments say what the code does: the ones that only said which
    Android or Material size, font or colour a value copies now say what
    the value is for, or are gone; references to the design notes are
    gone; Android class and method names give way to what the code does,
    and lengths are in GTK or screen pixels. No code changed.

 -- pulpoff <pashkovsky@gmail.com>  Fri, 13 Nov 2026 22:29:13 +0100

gnome-phone (0.1.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * neo-camera: a landscape layout. The shell turns the camera with the
    phone like any other app (the desktop file no longer asks for portrait):
    on its side the viewfinder is on the left and the controls stand in
    columns on the right, with the modes listed top to bottom. The preview
    keeps the picture upright for the back and the front camera whichever
    way the screen is turned; the camera reads the screen's rotation from
    Mutter's DisplayConfig.
  * extension: rotation.js's comments no longer name the camera as the app
    that stays portrait.
  * neo-settings: picking PIN as the screen lock type asks for a new PIN, as
    picking Pattern asks for a pattern; the type changes once the PIN is set.
    It kept the password there was, which the lock screen's PIN pad cannot
    type if it has a letter in it.

 -- pulpoff <pashkovsky@gmail.com>  Thu, 12 Nov 2026 17:05:33 +0100

gnome-phone (0.1.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * neo-weather: scrolls at the panel's rate (8.6 ms a frame on a 120 Hz
    phone, was 25 ms). The sky was a CSS gradient over the whole window,
    drawn again on every frame; it is a small texture stretched under the
    page now. The page scrolls with libspongy's fling and stretch, like the
    other apps.
  * neo-iconpack-round: the Round icon pack (Material Symbols glyphs on
    gradient discs, Apache-2.0), the launcher's default icons.
  * neo-browser: tabs live in one window. The URL line has a tab count that
    opens a switcher (a grid of the open tabs with thumbnails, close buttons,
    new tab and new incognito tab). New tab, target=_blank links and
    window.open open tabs in the same window; a tab's web view is made when
    the tab is first shown.
  * neo-weather, neo-settings: their own icons. They showed GNOME Weather's
    and GNOME Settings' icons, so with GNOME Weather removed (and with no
    icon pack) Weather had none.
  * neo-parallax-solar-system: a parallax wallpaper made from NASA's
    public-domain images (the Sun, Earth, the Moon, Mars, Saturn, Jupiter),
    the default wallpaper.
  * neo-camera: the cameras run only while the window is in focus. Recents
    shows windows live, so a camera left in the background opened its
    sensor every time recents opened (the focus motor clicked).
  * neo-dialer: comments name callaudiod's API, not one phone's daemon.
  * extension: a button tapped while the keyboard is up gets its tap. The
    keyboard closed (and the window grew back) as the finger came down, so
    the button moved away before it lifted: a dialog's Cancel or Rename did
    nothing and the dialog seemed to open again. The keyboard now closes
    once the finger lifts.
  * extension: the power key turns a panel without a low power mode (an
    LCD) off. It went black but stayed lit until the phone suspended.
  * neo-settings: a PIN, the current one and a new one, is typed on the
    lock screen's PIN pad, not the keyboard; a current password that is not
    digits has "Use password". A pattern is drawn as before.
  * neo-settings: Lock screen has an Always On Display switch on phones
    whose panel has a low power mode (OLED).
  * neo-settings: Language no longer asks localed for the system language,
    which Debian keeps read-only (the call was refused every time); the
    user's language is set as before and applies at the next log in.
  * neo-launcher: the home screen's grid is a setting: 4x5, 4x6, 5x5 (as
    before) or 5x6, with the icons' size (80 to 110 %) and their names on or
    off; the app drawer has 4 or 5 columns and an icon size of its own. A
    smaller grid moves the icons it has no cell for to the next free cells,
    on a new page if need be, and going back to a grid brings back its
    layout as it was. The screen follows a change at once. Values the GTK
    Neo left in these settings (4 columns, no search bar) are reset once,
    so nothing changes until Settings is used.
  * neo-launcher: the app drawer's search works. A tap on "Search apps"
    opens the keyboard and typing narrows the list to the apps whose name,
    generic name or keywords have a word starting with it; Back clears it.
    The hint is 16 sp, Launcher3's size (it was 20). With the search bar
    switched off the list starts where it was.
  * neo-settings: Home screen has a Layout group for the grid, the icon
    sizes, the app names, the app drawer's columns and its search bar.

 -- pulpoff <pashkovsky@gmail.com>  Thu, 12 Nov 2026 07:00:00 +0100

